     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet because the dose looks wrong.\n\n**The problem:** 1700 mg three times a day adds up to **5100 mg a day**. That's double the usual maximum for metformin:\n- Immediate-release: up to 2550 mg a day (for example, 850 mg three times daily)\n- Extended-release: up to 2000 mg a day\n\nA dose this high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and serious stomach side effects, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**Possible intended orders:**\n1. **850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals** (2550 mg a day, the immediate-release maximum). 1700 is exactly 2 \u00d7 850, so this seems the most likely mix-up.\n2. **1700 mg a day total**, given as 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n3. **Something else**, such as a lower starting dose if the patient is new to metformin. Doses usually start at 500 mg once or twice daily and go up slowly.\n\nTell me which dose and frequency you want, and whether it's immediate-release or extended-release, and I'll enter it right away. If you really did mean 1700 mg three times daily, please confirm that and give the reason, and I'll add it as a note on the order."
      }
     ],
